## What does this PR do?

Fixes WCAG 2.2.2 violation in `submissions/examples/ease-carousel/demo.html`.

The auto-play `setInterval` was starting unconditionally. Users who have enabled **prefers-reduced-motion** in their OS settings (to avoid vestibular-triggering animations) had no way to stop the carousel from auto-advancing.

## Changes

**File:** `submissions/examples/ease-carousel/demo.html` — JS block only.

```js
const reducedMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

function startAutoPlay() {
  if (reducedMotion.matches) return;  // skip when user prefers reduced motion
  timer = setInterval(...);
}

// Stop/start if the OS setting changes while the page is open
reducedMotion.addEventListener('change', function(e) {
  if (e.matches) stopAutoPlay();
  else startAutoPlay();
});
```

- Pause-on-hover (`mouseenter`/`mouseleave`) was already present — untouched.
- Pause-on-focus (`focusin`/`focusout`) was already present — untouched.
- Only the missing `prefers-reduced-motion` guard is added.

## WCAG reference

> **SC 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide (Level A):** For any auto-updating content that can be paused, a mechanism must be provided. An equivalent mechanism is to honour the `prefers-reduced-motion` OS preference and not start the motion at all.

