## 📄 Description

`document.querySelector('.newsletter-form')` returns only the **first** matching element on a page. Any additional `.newsletter-form` elements — e.g. a promotional banner form alongside the footer form — would silently receive no `submit` handler and fall back to a full browser page reload with no user feedback.

This is a latent defect: it works fine today because each page has only one newsletter form, but it will break as soon as a second form is added to any page.

**Fix:** Replace `querySelector` + `if` guard with `querySelectorAll` + `forEach`. Every `.newsletter-form` on every page now gets the handler, regardless of how many exist.

The email format validation (regex check + warning toast for invalid input) is also included here, making this the complete canonical version of the newsletter handler.

```js
// Before — only the first form gets the handler
const newsletterForm = document.querySelector('.newsletter-form');
if (newsletterForm) {
  newsletterForm.addEventListener('submit', function (e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const email = this.querySelector('input[type="email"]').value.trim();
    if (email) showToast('Thanks for subscribing!', 'success');
  });
}

// After — every form on the page gets the handler
const emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
document.querySelectorAll('.newsletter-form').forEach(function (form) {
  form.addEventListener('submit', function (e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const email = this.querySelector('input[type="email"]').value.trim();
    if (email && emailRegex.test(email)) {
      showToast('Thanks for subscribing!', 'success');
    } else {
      showToast('Please enter a valid email address.', 'warning');
    }
  });
});
```

> **Note for maintainer:** PR #2034 fixes only the email validation on the same block. This PR supersedes it with the complete fix. If both are open, closing #2034 and merging only this one is recommended to avoid a redundant diff.

---

