## Feature Name

`scroll-snap` + `animation-timeline: view()` Per-Section Entrance (Zero JS)

---

## Description

A full-page layout where `scroll-snap-type: y mandatory` snaps each section precisely into the viewport, and each section's content triggers its own entrance animation via the native CSS `animation-timeline: view()` API — no JavaScript, no Intersection Observer. `animation-range: entry 0% entry 60%` ensures the animation completes precisely as the section snaps into place.

Five content sections are included, each with a distinct entrance style:
1. Slide up + fade in
2. Scale in from 96% + blur clear
3. Slide in from right
4. Opacity stagger on child elements using `nth-child` delays
5. Exit animation using `animation-range: exit 0% exit 50%`

A `@supports (animation-timeline: view())` guard wraps the scroll-driven rules, with a fallback `@keyframes` that runs once on page load for unsupported browsers.

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

The scroll-driven animation submissions in the repository use `animation-timeline: view()` for freeform scrolling reveals, but none combine it with `scroll-snap-type`. When scroll-snap is active, the viewport snaps decisively to each section — making the view timeline progress advance from 0% to 100% reliably and in a controlled arc. This combination removes the need for JavaScript entirely for the most common "full-page portfolio with animated sections" layout pattern. The two APIs are complementary and the combination is genuinely underexplored.

---

## HTML Snippet

```html
<main class="snap-container">
  <section class="snap-section section-1">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>Section One</h2>
      <p>Slide up entrance via view timeline</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="snap-section section-2">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>Section Two</h2>
      <p>Scale in from below</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="snap-section section-3">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>Section Three</h2>
      <p>Slide in from the right</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="snap-section section-4">
    <div class="section-content stagger-children">
      <h2>Section Four</h2>
      <p class="child">Child one</p>
      <p class="child">Child two</p>
      <p class="child">Child three</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="snap-section section-5">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>Section Five</h2>
      <p>Exit animation on scroll out</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
```

---

## CSS Snippet

```css
/* ── Scroll snap container ── */
html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; }

.snap-container {
  height: 100vh;
  overflow-y: scroll;
  scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;
}

.snap-section {
  height: 100vh;
  scroll-snap-align: start;
  scroll-snap-stop: always;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}

/* ── Per-section entrance animations ── */
@keyframes slide-up-fade {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(40px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

@keyframes scale-blur-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.94); filter: blur(8px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; filter: none; }
}

@keyframes slide-from-right {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(60px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

@keyframes fade-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(20px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

/* ── View timeline bindings (requires browser support) ── */
@supports (animation-timeline: view()) {

  .section-1 .section-content {
    animation: slide-up-fade linear both;
    animation-timeline: view();
    animation-range: entry 0% entry 60%;
  }

  .section-2 .section-content {
    animation: scale-blur-in linear both;
    animation-timeline: view();
    animation-range: entry 0% entry 70%;
  }

  .section-3 .section-content {
    animation: slide-from-right linear both;
    animation-timeline: view();
    animation-range: entry 0% entry 65%;
  }

  /* Staggered children in section 4 */
  .section-4 .child {
    animation: fade-in linear both;
    animation-timeline: view();
    animation-range: entry 20% entry 80%;
  }
  .section-4 .child:nth-child(2) { animation-range: entry 30% entry 85%; }
  .section-4 .child:nth-child(3) { animation-range: entry 40% entry 90%; }

  /* Exit animation for section 5 */
  .section-5 .section-content {
    animation: slide-up-fade linear both;
    animation-timeline: view();
    animation-range: entry 0% exit 30%;
  }
}

/* ── Fallback: run once on load for unsupported browsers ── */
@supports not (animation-timeline: view()) {
  .section-content {
    animation: slide-up-fade 0.6s ease both;
  }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .section-content,
  .child {
    animation: none !important;
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    filter: none;
  }
}
```

---

## Visual Reference

As the user scrolls down and each section snaps into the viewport, its content animates into place. The snap guarantees the animation always completes cleanly — there is no partially-animated state visible mid-scroll. Section 5 also animates out as it exits the viewport, demonstrating the `exit` range. The entire interaction requires zero JavaScript.

---

## Checklist

- [x] This feature does not duplicate an existing EaseMotion CSS class
- [x] I understand my naming will be standardized by the maintainer
- [x] I will submit code inside `submissions/examples/` only — not in `core/` or `components/`

## Feature Name

`@property <length>` Individual Corner Radius Morphing for Organic Blobs

---

## Description

Registering each `border-radius` corner as a separate `@property` with `syntax: '<length>'` enables the browser to interpolate individual corner values independently and smoothly. Animating the `border-radius` shorthand directly (e.g. `60% 40% 30% 70% / 60% 30% 70% 40%`) produces unpredictable interpolation across browsers because the eight-value shorthand is resolved inconsistently. The `@property` approach gives deterministic, smooth per-corner animation.

Three demos are included:
1. **Side-by-side comparison** — shorthand animation (jumpy/inconsistent) vs `@property` per-corner animation (smooth and exact)
2. **Breathing blob cluster** — four blobs with `animation-delay` stagger creating an organic breathing group
3. **Hover morph** — element transitions from a rectangle to an organic shape on `:hover` using `transition` on the registered properties

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

The existing blob-morph submissions animate `border-radius` using the shorthand property directly. CSS cannot reliably interpolate the 8-value shorthand because it is computed as four independent length pairs — interpolation order is browser-dependent. Registering each of the four corners as `@property --r-tl`, `--r-tr`, `--r-bl`, `--r-br` typed as `<length>` forces predictable, smooth, independent per-corner interpolation and also enables `transition:` to work on corner values individually. This is the correct, future-proof approach to blob morphing in CSS.

---

## HTML Snippet

```html
<!-- Comparison: shorthand vs @property -->
<div class="morph-compare">
  <div>
    <p class="label">Shorthand animation</p>
    <div class="blob-shorthand"></div>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p class="label">@property per-corner</p>
    <div class="blob-typed"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Breathing cluster -->
<div class="blob-cluster">
  <div class="blob-typed" style="--delay:0s; --base-color:#6c63ff"></div>
  <div class="blob-typed" style="--delay:1.5s; --base-color:#22c55e"></div>
  <div class="blob-typed" style="--delay:3s; --base-color:#f59e0b"></div>
  <div class="blob-typed" style="--delay:4.5s; --base-color:#ef4444"></div>
</div>

<!-- Hover morph -->
<div class="blob-hover-demo">
  <div class="blob-morph-hover">Hover me</div>
</div>
```

---

## CSS Snippet

```css
/* Register each corner as a typed <length> property */
@property --r-tl { syntax: '<length>'; initial-value: 40px; inherits: false; }
@property --r-tr { syntax: '<length>'; initial-value: 40px; inherits: false; }
@property --r-bl { syntax: '<length>'; initial-value: 40px; inherits: false; }
@property --r-br { syntax: '<length>'; initial-value: 40px; inherits: false; }

.blob-typed {
  width: 160px;
  height: 160px;
  background: var(--base-color, #6c63ff);
  border-radius: var(--r-tl) var(--r-tr) var(--r-br) var(--r-bl);
  animation: blob-morph 6s ease-in-out infinite;
  animation-delay: var(--delay, 0s);
}

@keyframes blob-morph {
  0%, 100% { --r-tl: 60px; --r-tr: 30px; --r-bl: 20px; --r-br: 80px; }
  25%       { --r-tl: 20px; --r-tr: 90px; --r-bl: 70px; --r-br: 25px; }
  50%       { --r-tl: 80px; --r-tr: 20px; --r-bl: 40px; --r-br: 60px; }
  75%       { --r-tl: 35px; --r-tr: 65px; --r-bl: 80px; --r-br: 15px; }
}

/* Shorthand version for comparison (inconsistent browser behavior) */
