## Feature Name

`@property` Typed `<color>` Design Token Animation

---

## Description

CSS custom properties (variables) cannot be animated by default — the browser treats them as opaque strings and swaps their value at the 50% mark instead of interpolating. Registering a custom property with `@property` and `syntax: '<color>'` tells the browser the value is a color, enabling genuine smooth interpolation between any two color values, correctly computed in the `oklch` perceptual color space.

This submission demonstrates:
1. **Side-by-side comparison** — unregistered `--color` transitions (value snap at 50%) vs `@property` registered `--color` (smooth interpolation)
2. **Global brand token animation** — `--brand` cycling through three brand hues, with every UI element (button, badge, border, text accent) updating synchronously
3. **Hover state token** — button registers `@property --btn-bg: <color>` and uses it in `transition` for perceptually clean hover
4. **`oklch` midpoint advantage** — comparing `sRGB` midpoint (desaturated grey) vs `oklch` midpoint (vivid intermediate hue) for the same start and end colors

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

The existing `@property` submissions in the repository use `<angle>` and `<percentage>` syntax types for gradient animations. The `<color>` type is a separate capability with distinct practical value: it is the foundation for animating CSS design token systems. Without `@property` registration, `transition: color` on a custom property does nothing. This is a common point of confusion, and a well-structured demo makes the mechanism immediately clear.

---

## HTML Snippet

```html
<!-- Comparison: unregistered vs registered -->
<div class="token-demo">
  <div class="compare-col">
    <p class="label">Without @property</p>
    <button class="btn-unregistered">Hover me</button>
  </div>
  <div class="compare-col">
    <p class="label">With @property &lt;color&gt;</p>
    <button class="btn-registered">Hover me</button>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Global token animation: all elements consume --brand -->
<section class="brand-demo">
  <button class="brand-btn">Button</button>
  <span class="brand-badge">Badge</span>
  <div class="brand-card">Card with accent border</div>
</section>
```

---

## CSS Snippet

```css
/* Register the color token */
@property --brand {
  syntax: '<color>';
  initial-value: oklch(60% 0.25 270);
  inherits: true;
}

/* Animate the token at the :root level — all consumers update */
@keyframes brand-cycle {
  0%   { --brand: oklch(60% 0.25 270); } /* purple */
  33%  { --brand: oklch(65% 0.22 30);  } /* orange */
  66%  { --brand: oklch(70% 0.20 150); } /* teal */
  100% { --brand: oklch(60% 0.25 270); }
}

:root {
  animation: brand-cycle 9s ease-in-out infinite;
}

.brand-btn {
  background: var(--brand);
  transition: background 0.3s ease; /* works because --brand is registered */
}

.brand-badge {
  color: var(--brand);
  border-color: var(--brand);
}

.brand-card {
  border-left: 4px solid var(--brand);
}

/* Comparison: button with unregistered --color (snaps at 50%) */
.btn-unregistered {
  --color: #6c63ff;
  background: var(--color);
  transition: --color 0.5s ease; /* has no effect — browser can't interpolate */
}
.btn-unregistered:hover { --color: #ef4444; }

/* Button with @property <color> (smooth interpolation) */
@property --btn-bg {
  syntax: '<color>';
  initial-value: #6c63ff;
  inherits: false;
}
.btn-registered {
  background: var(--btn-bg);
  transition: --btn-bg 0.5s ease; /* works — browser interpolates correctly */
}
.btn-registered:hover { --btn-bg: #ef4444; }

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  :root { animation: none; }
}
```

---

## Visual Reference

In the comparison section: hover the unregistered button and the background jumps instantly at the 50% point. Hover the registered button and the background smoothly transitions through the intermediate hue. In the brand section, all elements shift through purple → orange → teal together as a synchronized design token system.

