## Feature Name

`:has()` Parent Selector Gallery Hover Cascade Animation

---

## Description

A CSS-only pattern where hovering any single child element causes all other sibling children to simultaneously dim, desaturate, and scale down — without a single line of JavaScript. This is achieved by using `:has(.card:hover)` on the parent container to select and de-emphasize all `.card:not(:hover)` siblings.

Three practical use cases are demonstrated:
1. **Image gallery** — hovering one image spotlights it while all others fade to greyscale
2. **Pricing cards** — the hovered plan elevates while competitors recede
3. **Product grid** — focused product stays vivid, surrounding ones desaturate and shrink

A `@supports selector(:has(*))` guard ensures clean degradation in browsers without `:has()` support.

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

`:has()` is the first CSS relational pseudo-class capable of selecting parents and preceding siblings. The gallery cascade pattern was previously impossible in pure CSS — it required JavaScript event delegation. This submission demonstrates the most practically impactful use of `:has()` for UI animation: cross-sibling state propagation on hover. Existing `:has()` submissions in the repository demonstrate only form-input state reveals, not this hover-cascade interaction model.

---

