## Feature Name

Multi-Plane `backdrop-filter` Depth-of-Field Effect

---

## Description

A simulated camera depth-of-field (DOF) effect using CSS `backdrop-filter: blur()` on multiple absolute-positioned overlay planes. Three content planes sit at different simulated z-distances: near, mid, and far. Selecting which plane is "in focus" via a set of radio buttons shifts the blur: the chosen plane goes to `blur(0px)` while the other two blur proportionally to their distance.

Distinct from `tilt-shift-depth-blur-sk` in the repo — that technique applies a fixed horizontal blur band across an image (simulating lens tilt-shift in photography). This submission simulates true depth-of-field: a focal plane that shifts between scene layers on user interaction, with blurred foreground elements growing (simulating bokeh) as they go out of focus.

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

`backdrop-filter` is widely used for frosted glass, but its potential for simulating depth is entirely unexplored in this repo. The multi-plane DOF effect adds genuine cinematic depth to hero sections and card UIs without any image manipulation, GPU-heavy canvas, or JavaScript. Three radio inputs drive the entire focal-plane shift through `:has()` selectors — a clean demonstration of both `backdrop-filter` animation and CSS `:has()` for stateful UI without JS.

The technique also demonstrates `will-change: backdrop-filter` for GPU compositing optimization, which is a relevant performance note for developers using this property.

---

