---

## Description

Most CSS list styling stops at `list-style-type`. This submission demonstrates that `::marker` pseudo-elements are independently animatable — you can apply `animation`, `color`, `font-size`, and `content` directly on the marker itself.

Three variants:
- **Staggered Entrance**: list items' markers pop into view one by one using `animation-delay: calc(var(--i) * 80ms)` — the marker scales and colorizes while the item text fades in
- **Checklist Toggle**: a `<ul>` where each `<li>` contains a checkbox. When checked, CSS `:has(input:checked)::marker` swaps `◦` → `✔` with a bounce keyframe — zero JavaScript
- **Ordered Progress List**: a numbered `<ol>` where `::marker` color interpolates from grey → indigo as items are visually "completed"

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

The `::marker` pseudo-element has been largely ignored in CSS animation demos. Developers routinely hide default markers and build custom bullets from `::before` elements — unnecessarily, because `::marker` is directly animatable. This submission fills that gap. It also demonstrates the `:has(input:checked)::marker` pattern which is a genuinely novel CSS-only interaction that replaces a common JavaScript use case. No submission in the repo currently animates `::marker`.

Important constraint documented in README: only `color`, `font-size`, and `content` are safe to animate on `::marker` per the current CSS spec. `transform` and `opacity` do **not** work on `::marker` and will be noted as pitfalls.

