## Feature Name

`overscroll-behavior` Scroll Containment Showcase

---

## Description

A side-by-side animated demo that visually explains all three values of the `overscroll-behavior` CSS property (`auto`, `contain`, `none`) using scrollable containers with CSS-animated visual indicators showing scroll-chain propagation.

- **`auto`**: when the inner scroll list reaches its end, animated arrows show the scroll event "leaking" out and propagating to the parent page
- **`contain`**: the arrows stop at the container boundary — a "BLOCKED" flash fires at the edge, visually confirming scroll containment
- **`none`**: neither chaining nor rubber-band bounce; the container hard-stops

The animated indicators are driven entirely by CSS , sibling  selectors, and  — no JavaScript.

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

`overscroll-behavior` is one of the most practically useful CSS properties for building modal dialogs, sidebars, command palettes, and bottom sheets — yet it is invisible by default and almost never demonstrated with motion. Developers continually rediscover the problem of scroll chaining (scrolling a modal scrolls the page behind it) and reach for JavaScript-based scroll-lock libraries. This demo shows the CSS-native solution with clear animated feedback, making the property immediately understandable. No equivalent submission exists in the repo.

---

