## Feature Name

CSS `clip-path: path()` Ink Blob Reveal — Organic Shape Expansion Animation

---

## Description

An element revealed not by a linear wipe or a geometric circle expansion, but by an **organic blob shape** that grows and morphs simultaneously using `clip-path: path('M...')` in `@keyframes`. The blob starts as a point, expands outward with irregular organic curves, and eventually covers the full element.

The critical technical requirement: all `path()` data in the keyframe chain must have **identical vertex counts** for the browser to interpolate between them smoothly. This is the constraint that makes the effect hard to get right and rewarding when it works.

This is distinct from `clip-path-transitions` submissions (which use `circle()`, `inset()`, `polygon()`) — those use CSS predefined shape primitives. This uses raw SVG path data for true organic shapes.

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

Page reveals, hover reveals, and content transitions are among the most common animation needs. An ink-spread blob reveal is immediately recognisable as premium motion design — it appears in high-end agency sites and app onboarding flows. Providing a CSS-only version makes the pattern accessible without JavaScript.

---

