## 🐞 Description

In `track-order.js`, the `renderResult()` function creates a `setInterval` timer (`progressTimer`) to animate the simulated delivery progress bar. The timer is declared with `const` **inside** `renderResult()` — it is locally scoped and completely inaccessible from `resetTracker()`. When the user clicks "Track Another Order", the timer is never cancelled. Every subsequent order lookup spawns an additional interval while all previous ones keep running in the background.

After N lookups: N independent intervals are all calling `getElementById("liveProgressBar")` and updating the same DOM node every 3 seconds indefinitely. The intervals only self-terminate if `currentPct >= 99`, which can take many minutes. If the user tracks multiple orders in a session, the intervals accumulate silently.

---

## 🔁 Steps to Reproduce

1. Open `track-order.html`
2. Enter order ID `CARA-20261234` + any email → submit
3. Watch the progress bar animate
4. Click "Track Another Order"
5. Submit the same order again
6. Open DevTools → Performance tab → record for 10 seconds
7. Observe multiple overlapping `setInterval` callbacks firing every 3s — one per lookup

You can also verify by patching before loading the page:
```js
const orig = window.setInterval;
let count = 0;
window.setInterval = (...args) => { count++; console.log('Active intervals:', count); return orig(...args); };
```
After two order lookups without page reload, count is 2+.

---

## 💻 Expected Behavior

`resetTracker()` cancels the existing progress bar interval before returning the user to the form. Each new `renderResult()` call cancels any previous interval before creating a new one. At most one interval runs at any time.

---

## 🧠 Actual Behavior

Current code in `renderResult()`:

```js
// progressTimer is const-scoped inside renderResult — unreachable from outside
let currentPct = 62;
const progressTimer = setInterval(() => {
    if (currentPct < 99) {
        currentPct += (Math.random() * 0.5 + 0.1);
        // updates DOM elements
    } else {
        clearInterval(progressTimer);
    }
}, 3000);
```

And `resetTracker()` has no reference to `progressTimer` at all:

```js
function resetTracker() {
    formCard.style.display = "block";
    resultCard.style.display = "none";
    errorCard.style.display = "none";
    // progressTimer is never cleared
}
```

