### Description

`GamificationDashboard` creates `setTimeout` callbacks inside `handleAction` to auto-dismiss achievement toasts, but never stores or cancels the timer IDs. If the user navigates away before the 4-second window expires, the timers still fire and call `setToasts` on a stale closure.

```jsx
// website/src/components/gamification/GamificationDashboard.jsx lines 30–38
newToasts.forEach((t) => {
  setTimeout(() => {                            // ID discarded immediately
    setToasts((prev) => prev.filter((x) => x.id !== t.id));
  }, 4000);
});
```

Timer IDs are never stored in a ref, so there is no way to cancel them in a cleanup function. With rapid action clicks, multiple timers accumulate — each holding a closure reference to `setToasts` and the previous toasts array. React 18 does not throw on setState after unmount, so this fails silently while leaking memory for up to 4 seconds per batch.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Open the Gamification Hub
2. Click several actions quickly to trigger multiple achievement toasts
3. Immediately navigate to another page before 4 seconds pass
4. Check the React DevTools or browser console — `setToasts` fires on the unmounted component

### Expected Behavior

All pending timers should be cancelled when the component unmounts. A `useRef` tracking timer IDs with a `useEffect` cleanup handles this:

```jsx
const timerIds = useRef([]);

useEffect(() => {
  return () => timerIds.current.forEach(clearTimeout);
}, []);

// in handleAction:
const id = setTimeout(() => {
  setToasts((prev) => prev.filter((x) => x.id !== t.id));
}, 4000);
timerIds.current.push(id);
```

