## Description

\`startRecording\` in \`client/src/modules/mock-interview/hooks/useInterviewAudio.js\` has no guard against concurrent invocations. The function is \`async\` and the very first thing it does is \`await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ audio: true })\`, which takes 100–500ms. During that window, \`isRecording\` is still \`false\` (it's only set to \`true\` at the end of the function), so a second call can enter freely.

When two calls overlap:
1. Both \`getUserMedia\` requests resolve with separate \`MediaStream\` instances
2. \`mediaRecorderRef.current\` is overwritten by the second call
3. \`mediaStreamRef.current\` is overwritten by the second call
4. The first stream's tracks are never stopped — the browser microphone indicator stays on after recording ends

\`\`\`js
const startRecording = async () => {
  // no guard here — isRecording is still false while awaiting getUserMedia
  try {
    const stream = await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ audio: true });
    mediaRecorderRef.current = mediaRecorder; // second call overwrites this
    mediaStreamRef.current = stream;          // first stream is now lost
    // ...
    setIsRecording(true); // too late to prevent the race
  }
};
\`\`\`

\`stopRecording\` and \`cleanupAudio\` both operate on \`mediaRecorderRef.current\` — whichever call "won" the overwrite. The first stream's tracks are never stopped.

This is a privacy concern: the microphone stays active (browser indicator stays on) after the interview session ends.

## Reproduction Steps

1. Go to a mock interview session page
2. Double-click the "Start Recording" button quickly (or trigger it twice in rapid succession via keyboard)
3. Open DevTools > Application > Camera/Microphone permissions — two active streams are visible
4. Click "Stop Recording" — one stream stops, the other remains active

## Expected Behavior

A second call to \`startRecording\` while one is already in-flight (or already recording) should be a no-op. Only one \`MediaStream\` should ever be open at a time.

## Screenshots / Logs

No console error — the bug is silent. Symptom is the browser's microphone access indicator remaining active after stopping/ending the interview.

## Environment Details

- Chrome / Firefox (any browser with getUserMedia)
- Affects the mock interview feature

## Additional Context

Fix is to add an in-flight ref at the top of the hook:

\`\`\`js
const isStartingRef = useRef(false);

const startRecording = async () => {
  if (isStartingRef.current || isRecording) return;
  isStartingRef.current = true;
  try {
    // existing logic
  } finally {
    isStartingRef.current = false;
  }
};
\`\`\`

The function should also be wrapped in \`useCallback\` with proper deps so the UI button can reliably disable itself during the async window.

