## Description

Two related bugs in \`ClassroomRoom.jsx\` that together can crash the room and leave the user's camera/mic permanently open.

**Bug 1 — null pointer crash in cleanup:**
When a remote user joins, a placeholder peer object is pushed to \`peersRef.current\` with \`peer: null\` (line 137/143) before the WebRTC handshake completes. If the local user unmounts the component (navigates away, leaves) before the \`webrtc-offer\` arrives and \`addPeer\` fills in the real \`Peer\` instance, the cleanup function at line 282 throws:

\`\`\`
peersRef.current.forEach((p) => p.peer.destroy());
// TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'destroy')
\`\`\`

**Bug 2 — MediaStream orphan on early unmount:**
\`getUserMedia\` is called inside \`useEffect\` but the cleanup return function runs synchronously before the promise resolves. If the component unmounts quickly (e.g., user leaves within ~200ms of opening the room), cleanup fires first — \`localStreamRef.current\` is still \`null\` so nothing is stopped. The \`.then(stream => {...})\` callback then fires on the already-unmounted component, sets \`localStreamRef.current = stream\`, and registers socket event listeners on the now-disconnected socket. The stream is never stopped — the browser camera/microphone indicator stays on for the rest of the session.

## Reproduction Steps

1. Open a classroom room while another participant is joining at the same time
2. Click "Leave" immediately before the WebRTC handshake completes
3. See TypeError in console: \`Cannot read properties of null (reading 'destroy')\`

For bug 2: navigate away within ~200ms of joining — the browser camera indicator will remain active.

## Expected Behavior

Cleanup should safely guard null peer references, and any stream acquired after unmount should be immediately stopped.

## Screenshots / Logs

\`\`\`
TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'destroy')
    at ClassroomRoom.jsx:282
\`\`\`

## Environment Details

- Affects all environments
- Reproducible in Chrome and Firefox

## Additional Context

The fix for bug 1 is straightforward — change line 282 to \`p.peer?.destroy()\`.

For bug 2, a mounted flag is needed:

\`\`\`js
let isMounted = true;
navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ video: true, audio: true })
  .then((stream) => {
    if (!isMounted) {
      stream.getTracks().forEach((t) => t.stop());
      return;
    }
    // rest of handler
  });
return () => {
  isMounted = false;
  // existing cleanup
};
\`\`\`

