---

## Description

CSS trig functions (`sin()`, `cos()`) shipped in Chrome 111, Firefox 108, Safari 15.4 and are now broadly available. This submission uses them to position loader dots on mathematically-computed paths — no JS, no SVG transforms. A single `--t` custom property animates from `0turn` to `1turn`, and each dot's position is calculated as `translateX(calc(sin(var(--t)) * 60px))` with Y using `cos(calc(var(--t) * 2))` — varying the frequency ratio produces different Lissajous figures (2:1, 3:2, 1:1).

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

There is no submission in this repo that uses CSS trig functions for actual motion path computation. Every loader here either uses `border-radius` tricks or `transform: rotate()` on wrapper elements. Lissajous loaders are visually distinct and demonstrate a CSS capability that most developers don't know exists yet.

