### Description

`server/routes/notifications.js` (lines 177–185) exposes a completely unauthenticated `GET /api/notifications` endpoint. The `userId` used to scope the response is taken directly from the query string with no validation against an auth token:

```js
router.get('/api/notifications', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const userId = req.query.userId || 'global';   // ← no auth middleware, no ownership check
    const list = await notificationsService.getNotifications(userId);
    return res.json({ notifications: list });
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(500).json({ error: err.message });
  }
});
```

Every sibling write endpoint in the same file (`DELETE /api/notifications`, `POST /api/notifications/mark-read`, etc.) correctly requires `adminAuth`. The GET endpoint has no middleware at all.

This means any unauthenticated HTTP client can read another user's notification feed by supplying a known or guessed `userId`:

```
GET /api/notifications?userId=<victim-user-id>
→ 200 OK { notifications: [ ...all of victim's notifications... ] }
```

Notifications contain sensitive content: event registration confirmations, waitlist promotions, attendance marks, and admin announcements addressed to specific users. This is an unauthenticated Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR).

This is a separate and distinct issue from [#1902](https://github.com/Ayushh-Sharmaa/NexaSphere/issues/1902), which reported that write routes were incorrectly blocked by `adminAuth`. This issue is about the read route having no auth at all.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Obtain or guess a `userId` (user IDs are present in client-side decoded JWT payloads and visible in browser storage).
2. Without any authentication headers, send:
   ```
   GET /api/notifications?userId=<target-user-id>
   ```
3. Receive `200 OK` with the target user's full notification list.

### Expected Behavior

`GET /api/notifications` should require a valid auth token and only return notifications belonging to the authenticated user. The server must validate that `req.query.userId` matches the identity from the verified token.

### Suggested Fix

Add authentication middleware and an ownership check:

```js
import { studentAuthMiddleware } from '../middleware/studentAuthMiddleware.js';

router.get('/api/notifications', studentAuthMiddleware.requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const authenticatedUserId = req.user?.sub || req.user?.id;
    const requestedUserId = req.query.userId || 'global';

    if (requestedUserId !== 'global' && requestedUserId !== authenticatedUserId) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
    }

    const list = await notificationsService.getNotifications(authenticatedUserId);
    return res.json({ notifications: list });
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(500).json({ error: err.message });
  }
});
```

