### Description

`website/src/components/ResumeAnalyzer/ResumeUploader.jsx` displays the hint text `"PDF, DOC, or DOCX · Max 5MB"` (line 57) but the `handleFile` function never checks `file.size`. Any file, regardless of size, is accepted and forwarded directly to `onUpload()`.

```js
const handleFile = (f) => {
  if (!f) return;
  const allowed = [
    'application/pdf',
    'application/msword',
    'application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document',
  ];
  if (!allowed.includes(f.type)) {
    setError('Please upload a PDF or DOC/DOCX file.');
    return;
  }
  // ← No file.size check. A 200 MB file passes through silently.
  setError('');
  setFile(f);
  onUpload(f);
};
```

This means a user can upload a 50 MB or 200 MB resume and `onUpload` will be called without any warning. The UI hint is misleading and the AI backend (or any network layer that processes the file) will receive unexpectedly large payloads, leading to timeouts, increased costs, and potentially unhelpful error messages that surface with no context.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Navigate to the Resume Analyzer page.
2. Drag and drop (or click to select) any file larger than 5 MB that matches one of the allowed MIME types (PDF, DOC, DOCX).
3. Observe that no error is shown and `onUpload` is called with the oversized file.

### Expected Behavior

Files exceeding 5 MB should be rejected with a clear error message: `"File must be 5 MB or smaller."` The upload should not proceed.

### Suggested Fix

Add a size check immediately after the MIME type check in `handleFile`:

```js
const MAX_SIZE = 5 * 1024 * 1024; // 5 MB

const handleFile = (f) => {
  if (!f) return;
  const allowed = [...];
  if (!allowed.includes(f.type)) {
    setError('Please upload a PDF or DOC/DOCX file.');
    return;
  }
  if (f.size > MAX_SIZE) {
    setError('File must be 5 MB or smaller.');
    return;
  }
  setError('');
  setFile(f);
  onUpload(f);
};
```

