### Description

The `Typewriter` component defined inside `website/src/pages/events/EventDetailPage.jsx` (lines 11–36) creates a `setInterval` inside an `IntersectionObserver` callback. Because the interval handle `t` is scoped inside that callback, it is completely invisible to the `useEffect` cleanup function, which only calls `obs.disconnect()`. When the `text` or `speed` prop changes (triggering an effect re-run) or the component unmounts while the typing animation is still in progress, the old interval continues firing indefinitely.

```js
useEffect(() => {
  const obs = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => {
    if (e.isIntersecting && !started.current) {
      started.current = true;
      let i = 0;
      const t = setInterval(() => {   // ← t is scoped inside callback
        setDisplayed(text.slice(0, i + 1));
        i++;
        if (i >= text.length) { setDone(true); clearInterval(t); }
      }, speed);
    }
  }, { threshold: 0.3 });
  if (ref.current) obs.observe(ref.current);
  return () => obs.disconnect();   // ← t is never cleared here
}, [text, speed]);
```

Every navigation to the event detail page while a previous animation is running stacks another orphan interval. This causes state updates on unmounted components and steadily increases memory usage over a session.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Open an event detail page that has a long overview text (the `Typewriter` component is rendered at line 823).
2. While the typing animation is playing, navigate away (back button or another route).
3. Observe console warnings about state updates on unmounted components, and note that CPU usage from the orphan interval persists.

Alternatively: change the `text` or `speed` prop mid-animation (e.g. by switching events) — the old interval keeps running alongside the new one.

### Expected Behavior

When the component unmounts or the effect re-runs, all resources (both the `IntersectionObserver` and the `setInterval`) are cleaned up so no further state updates occur and no memory is leaked.

### Suggested Fix

Capture the interval ID in a `useRef` so it is accessible to the cleanup closure:

```js
const intervalRef = useRef(null);

useEffect(() => {
  const obs = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => {
    if (e.isIntersecting && !started.current) {
      started.current = true;
      let i = 0;
      intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {
        setDisplayed(text.slice(0, i + 1));
        i++;
        if (i >= text.length) {
          setDone(true);
          clearInterval(intervalRef.current);
        }
      }, speed);
    }
  }, { threshold: 0.3 });
  if (ref.current) obs.observe(ref.current);
  return () => {
    obs.disconnect();
    clearInterval(intervalRef.current);
  };
}, [text, speed]);
```

