- Framework: React (Vite)
- Affects all browsers

## Description

`extractApiMessage` in `client/src/services/apiClient.js` (lines 19–45) is a mutually recursive function that traverses nested error objects (`value.message`, `value.msg`, `value.detail`, `value.error`, `value.details`) with no depth guard. 

\`\`\`js
const extractApiMessage = (value) => {
  if (typeof value === "string") return value;

  if (Array.isArray(value)) {
    for (const item of value) {
      const message = extractApiMessage(item); // unbounded recursion
      if (message) return message;
    }
    return null;
  }

  if (!isPlainObject(value)) return null;

  return (
    extractApiMessage(value.message) ||   // unbounded recursion
    extractApiMessage(value.msg)     ||   // unbounded recursion
    extractApiMessage(value.detail)  ||   // unbounded recursion
    extractApiMessage(value.error)   ||   // unbounded recursion — self-referential objects crash here
    extractApiMessage(value.details)      // unbounded recursion
  );
};
\`\`\`

**Crash scenarios:**

1. **Self-referential error object:** If any field of an API error body points back to itself (e.g., `err.error = err`), the function recurses infinitely and throws `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`, crashing the entire React application.

2. **Deeply nested error from external services:** The Python AI-ML microservice or the Gemini API may return deeply nested error structures (e.g., `{ error: { error: { error: { message: "..." } } } }`) that exhaust the call stack before reaching the string leaf.

This function is called on every failed API request (`normalizeApiError` at line ~200), meaning any sufficiently malformed error response from any backend service can bring down the client-side app.

## Reproduction Steps

1. Modify any API endpoint to return a response body like:
   \`\`\`json
   { "error": { "error": { "error": { "error": { ... } } } } }
   \`\`\`
   (or create a circular reference programmatically in a test)
2. Trigger a request from the frontend that hits that endpoint
3. Observe: `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` thrown inside `extractApiMessage`, React error boundary catches it and the entire page falls back to the error UI

## Expected Behavior

`extractApiMessage` should return `null` (gracefully) when the error object is deeper than a reasonable threshold (e.g., 10 levels), rather than throwing a stack overflow.

## Screenshots / Logs

\`\`\`
RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded
    at extractApiMessage (apiClient.js:19)
    at extractApiMessage (apiClient.js:39)
    at extractApiMessage (apiClient.js:39)
    ...
\`\`\`

## Environment Details

- File: `client/src/services/apiClient.js` (lines 19–45)
- Triggered by: any failed API response with circular or deeply nested error body
- Browser: all

## Additional Context

**Suggested fix** — add a depth parameter with a maximum:

\`\`\`js
const extractApiMessage = (value, depth = 0) => {
  if (depth > 10) return null;   // guard against infinite recursion

  if (typeof value === "string") return value;

  if (Array.isArray(value)) {
    for (const item of value) {
      const message = extractApiMessage(item, depth + 1);
      if (message) return message;
    }
    return null;
  }

  if (!isPlainObject(value)) return null;

  return (
    extractApiMessage(value.message, depth + 1) ||
    extractApiMessage(value.msg,     depth + 1) ||
    extractApiMessage(value.detail,  depth + 1) ||
    extractApiMessage(value.error,   depth + 1) ||
    extractApiMessage(value.details, depth + 1)
  );
};
\`\`\`

The threshold of `10` is conservative — real-world API error messages are never nested more than 3–4 levels deep.

## Current Problem

The security header middleware in `server/index.js` (lines 143–148) only sets two headers:

\`\`\`js
res.setHeader("Referrer-Policy", "no-referrer");
res.setHeader("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff");
\`\`\`

The following production-grade security headers are entirely absent:

| Missing Header | Attack it prevents |
|---|---|
| `X-Frame-Options: DENY` | Clickjacking — embedding the app in a malicious iframe |
| `Strict-Transport-Security` | SSL stripping, protocol downgrade / MITM |
| `Content-Security-Policy` | XSS, data injection, resource hijacking |
| `Permissions-Policy` | Unauthorised access to camera, microphone, geolocation |
| `X-XSS-Protection: 0` | Disables broken legacy browser XSS auditor that can itself be exploited |

This application handles sensitive PII (resumes, personal names, emails — all encrypted at rest in MongoDB) and issues JWT tokens for authentication. Missing these headers leaves the app exposed to well-known, trivially-exploitable browser-level attacks.

## Proposed Solution

Add the missing headers to the existing security header middleware in `server/index.js`:

\`\`\`js
// server/index.js — lines 143–148
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.setHeader("Referrer-Policy", "no-referrer");
  res.setHeader("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff");
  res.setHeader("X-Frame-Options", "DENY");
  res.setHeader("X-XSS-Protection", "0");
  res.setHeader(
    "Strict-Transport-Security",
    "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
  );
  res.setHeader(
    "Content-Security-Policy",
    "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; connect-src 'self'"
  );
  res.setHeader(
    "Permissions-Policy",
    "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()"
  );
  next();
});
\`\`\`

Alternatively, replace the manual middleware with the `helmet` package which handles all of these correctly and is kept up-to-date with evolving browser security standards:

\`\`\`bash
npm install helmet
\`\`\`

\`\`\`js
import helmet from "helmet";
app.use(helmet());
\`\`\`

## Expected Impact

- Prevents clickjacking attacks against authenticated users
- Enforces HTTPS-only transport for all browsers that have visited the app
- Provides a first line of defence against XSS by restricting resource origins
- Prevents the app from requesting browser hardware (camera/mic/geolocation) without explicit user permission
- Passes security audits (OWASP ZAP, Mozilla Observatory) which are common checks for production deployments

## Implementation Ideas

1. Add the missing `res.setHeader` calls to the existing middleware block in `server/index.js` — minimal change
2. Or install and configure `helmet` for a maintained, comprehensive solution
3. The CSP policy should be tuned to match the actual CDN/resource origins used in the client (e.g., Google Fonts, Cloudinary image URLs)

## Alternatives Considered

Leaving the current two-header setup is insufficient for production. The headers above are the baseline recommended by OWASP and the Mozilla Web Security Guidelines for any app handling user authentication and PII.

### Description

The `SuggestedPartners` component renders each recommended peer's full email address as visible text on the page. Every logged-in user who visits the dashboard can read the private email addresses of other platform members without their knowledge or consent.

### Affected File

- `src/components/SuggestedPartners.tsx` — line 61

### Root Cause

```tsx
interface Partner {
  _id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;          // ← email included in type
  skills: string[];
  interests: string[];
  compatibilityScore: number;
}

// ...rendered in JSX:
<p className="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-2">
  {partner.email}         {/* ← displayed to every authenticated user */}
</p>
```

The `Partner` interface includes `email`, and the component renders it plainly beneath the partner's name. Email addresses are personal data — exposing them to other users without consent violates basic privacy expectations and data-minimisation principles under GDPR Article 5.

### Impact

- Any authenticated user can harvest the email addresses of all other users simply by visiting the dashboard.
- Users who signed up with a personal or work email address have not consented to it being shared with peers.
- Reputational and legal risk for the platform.

### Proposed Fix

Remove `email` from the `Partner` interface and delete the rendering line. If peer contact is required, it should be routed through the existing in-app messaging system:

```ts
interface Partner {
  _id: string;
  name: string;
  // email removed
  skills: string[];
  interests: string[];
  compatibilityScore: number;
}
```

Also verify that the backend `matchController` does not return `email` in the recommendations payload so the data is not transmitted at all.

### Description

The `FocusTimer` component's countdown `useEffect` calls `handleTimerComplete()` when `timeLeft` reaches zero, but `handleTimerComplete` is not listed as a dependency. React captures the version of `handleTimerComplete` that existed at the last render where `isActive` or `timeLeft` changed — not the current render. This means the function runs with stale copies of `isBreak`, `workDuration`, `breakDuration`, and `focusTimeThisWeek`.

### Affected File

- `src/components/FocusTimer.tsx` — timer `useEffect` (lines ~44–57) and `handleTimerComplete` (~lines 59–86)

### Root Cause

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  let interval: NodeJS.Timeout;
  if (isActive && timeLeft > 0) {
    interval = setInterval(() => setTimeLeft((prev) => prev - 1), 1000);
  } else if (isActive && timeLeft === 0) {
    handleTimerComplete(); // closes over stale isBreak, workDuration, breakDuration, focusTimeThisWeek
  }
  return () => clearInterval(interval);
}, [isActive, timeLeft]); // handleTimerComplete is missing from deps
```

`handleTimerComplete` reads `isBreak` to decide the branch, `workDuration` for the toast message and for computing `newFocusTime`, and `breakDuration` for the next `setTimeLeft` call. None of these are in the dep array, so their captured values can be stale.

### Impact

- If the user adjusts `workDuration` or `breakDuration` after starting the timer, the completion handler uses the old values — wrong toast message, wrong break duration set, wrong focus time written to the database.
- If `isBreak` is stale (e.g., the component re-rendered for an unrelated reason), the wrong branch executes: a work-complete handler fires during a break or vice-versa, permanently misaligning the timer state.
- `focusTimeThisWeek + workDuration` accumulates incorrect minutes in the `profiles` table.

### Proposed Fix

Wrap `handleTimerComplete` in `useCallback` with its true dependencies, then add it to the `useEffect` dep array:

```ts
const handleTimerComplete = useCallback(async () => {
  if (!isBreak) {
    // ... work complete logic using current isBreak, workDuration, breakDuration
  } else {
    // ... break complete logic
  }
}, [isBreak, workDuration, breakDuration, focusTimeThisWeek, user]);

useEffect(() => {
  let interval: NodeJS.Timeout;
  if (isActive && timeLeft > 0) {
    interval = setInterval(() => setTimeLeft((prev) => prev - 1), 1000);
  } else if (isActive && timeLeft === 0) {
    handleTimerComplete();
  }
  return () => clearInterval(interval);
}, [isActive, timeLeft, handleTimerComplete]);
```

### Description

Once the group Pomodoro timer reaches zero, `handleTimerComplete` is called on every subsequent interval tick (every second) until the resulting Supabase state update propagates back and triggers a re-render. Because `diff` is clamped to `0` by `Math.max`, it never goes negative, so the `if (diff === 0)` guard fires repeatedly rather than exactly once.

### Affected File

- `src/components/GroupPomodoro.tsx` — countdown `useEffect`, lines ~67–78

### Root Cause

```ts
interval = setInterval(() => {
  const diff = Math.max(0, Math.floor((endTime.getTime() - now.getTime()) / 1000));
  setTimeLeft(diff);

  if (diff === 0) {
    handleTimerComplete(); // ← called every tick once diff is permanently 0
  }
}, 1000);
```

After expiry `diff` stays `0` on every tick. `handleTimerComplete` issues a `supabase.update()` call each time, so on a 200 ms network round-trip the break timer can be written 4–5 times before the state change unsubscribes the interval.

### Impact

- Multiple redundant Supabase `UPDATE` calls per timer completion, wasting database quota and increasing latency for all room participants.
- Race condition: simultaneous writes can set conflicting `timer_end_time` values, causing the break timer to start at a slightly wrong offset.
- In degraded-network conditions the timer can enter a broken state visible to all users in the shared study room.

### Proposed Fix

Clear the interval inside the callback before calling `handleTimerComplete`, or use a ref guard to ensure the handler fires at most once:

```ts
interval = setInterval(() => {
  const diff = Math.max(0, Math.floor((endTime.getTime() - now.getTime()) / 1000));
  setTimeLeft(diff);

  if (diff === 0) {
    clearInterval(interval); // stop further ticks immediately
    handleTimerComplete();
  }
}, 1000);
```

