window.location.href = `/api/assessments/export.csv?${buildExportParams().toString()}`;
```

When the server returns an error response — 401 (session expired), 429 (rate limited), or 500 (database error) — the browser navigates to the error URL and renders the raw JSON error body (e.g. `{"message":"Failed to export data"}`) as a blank or broken page. The user has no way to return to their history view without pressing Back.

Additionally:
- There is no loading/in-progress indicator while the export is being generated.
- If the session has expired, the user is not redirected to login — they see a raw 401 JSON response instead.
- The URL including all filter parameters is exposed in the browser's navigation history.

**To Reproduce**

1. Open the History page
2. Let the session expire (or manually clear the session cookie)
3. Click the "Export CSV" button
4. Browser navigates to `/api/assessments/export.csv?...` and displays `{"message":"Unauthorized"}` as plain text

**Expected behavior**

Export errors should be shown as in-app toast notifications. The user should remain on the History page. A loading state should be shown while the download is in progress.

**Suggested fix**

Replace the navigation with a `fetch`-based download:

```ts
async function handleExport() {
  setExporting(true);
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/assessments/export.csv?${buildExportParams()}`, {
      credentials: "include",
    });
    if (!res.ok) {
      const err = await res.json().catch(() => ({ message: "Export failed" }));
      toast({ title: "Export failed", description: err.message, variant: "destructive" });
      return;
    }
    const blob = await res.blob();
    const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    const a = document.createElement("a");
    a.href = url;
    a.download = "assessments.csv";
    a.click();
    URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
  } finally {
    setExporting(false);
  }
}
```

**Additional context**

The PDF export in the same file was already updated to use the Blob URL + anchor pattern (see the comment at line ~440 referencing the fix for `document.write`). The CSV export should follow the same approach for consistency.

Note: Issue #985 covers the PDF export (`exportAsPdf`) using `window.alert` and `document.write`. This is a separate, unreported issue affecting the CSV export path.

**Describe the bug**

In `client/src/components/WhatIfRiskSimulator.tsx`, a `useEffect` calls `whatIfBatchMutation.mutate(...)` on component mount with an empty dependency array `[]`. The payload is built from `values` (a state variable) and `buildInput(values)` — neither of which is listed in the dependency array.

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  const perturbations = [ { bmi: 25 }, { hba1cLevel: 5.7 }, ... ];
  whatIfBatchMutation.mutate(
    { original: buildInput(values), perturbations },  // `values` captured at mount time
    { onSuccess: (data) => setBatchResult(data), onError: () => {} }
  );
}, []);  // ← missing deps: values, buildInput, whatIfBatchMutation
```

This causes two problems:

**1. Stale closure — wrong patient data sent to ML**

When `WhatIfRiskSimulator` is rendered with a pre-loaded assessment (e.g., when a provider opens an existing record), the `values` state is populated from props asynchronously. The `useEffect` fires before React has applied all prop-derived state updates, so `buildInput(values)` produces default/empty values instead of the actual patient data. The ML service receives incorrect inputs and the scenario comparison panel shows wrong results.

**2. Batch results never update when user changes parameters**

Because the deps array is empty, the batch simulation never re-runs after mount. If a user adjusts the BMI or HbA1c slider in the simulator and then looks at the "scenario recommendations" section, the comparison is still against the stale initial values from mount.

**To Reproduce**

1. Open an existing assessment in the dashboard
2. The WhatIfRiskSimulator renders with the assessment's data
3. Observe the "scenario comparison" section — it shows results based on default form values, not the loaded patient's actual values
4. Change any slider value — the batch comparison does not update

**Expected behavior**

The batch simulation should run with the current `values` state, and should re-run whenever `values` change (with appropriate debouncing to avoid excessive API calls).

**Suggested fix**

Option A — run batch inside the explicit simulate handler (simplest):
Remove the `useEffect` entirely and call `whatIfBatchMutation.mutate(...)` at the end of the `handleSimulate` function after the single simulation completes.

Option B — debounced effect:
```ts
useEffect(() => {
  const timeout = setTimeout(() => {
    whatIfBatchMutation.mutate(
      { original: buildInput(values), perturbations },
      { onSuccess: (data) => setBatchResult(data), onError: () => {} }
    );
  }, 500);
  return () => clearTimeout(timeout);
// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, [values]);
```

**Additional context**

The `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` ESLint rule would flag this. Running `eslint client/src/components/WhatIfRiskSimulator.tsx` should reproduce the warning.

### Description

`upsertBriefOverlay()` in `src/content.ts:261-305` creates a floating overlay card injected into the Google Meet page to display the late-joiner brief. The root container element is a plain `<div>` with no ARIA role or modal attributes:

```ts
overlay = document.createElement("div");
overlay.id = overlayId;
// no role, no aria-modal, no aria-labelledby
```

This violates WCAG 2.1 Success Criteria:
- **SC 4.1.2** (Name, Role, Value): The element has no `role="dialog"`, so assistive technologies cannot identify it as a dialog.
- **SC 2.4.3** (Focus Order): Focus is not moved to the overlay when it appears. Keyboard-only users cannot interact with it without tabbing through the entire Meet UI first.
- **SC 1.3.1** (Info and Relationships): The title element is not programmatically associated with the dialog via `aria-labelledby`.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Join a meeting late so the brief overlay is triggered.
2. Use a screen reader (VoiceOver, NVDA) and observe that the overlay is not announced as a dialog.
3. Use keyboard-only navigation and observe that Tab does not move focus into the overlay automatically.

### Expected Behavior

The overlay should be announced as a modal dialog by screen readers, focus should move to the close button or first interactive element on open, and focus should be returned to the previously focused element on close.

### Suggested Fix

```ts
overlay = document.createElement("div");
overlay.id = overlayId;
overlay.setAttribute("role", "dialog");
overlay.setAttribute("aria-modal", "true");
overlay.setAttribute("aria-labelledby", "mc-brief-title-label");

// give the title element a matching id
title.id = "mc-brief-title-label";

// move focus to the close button when the overlay opens
requestAnimationFrame(() => closeBtn.focus());
```

The existing `closeOverlay` function already removes the element; restore focus to the previously focused element there:

```ts
const previousFocus = document.activeElement as HTMLElement | null;
const closeOverlay = () => {
  overlay?.remove();
  previousFocus?.focus();
};
```

