## 🐛 What's broken?

`scripts/check-duplicates.mjs` uses a regex that only detects a class selector when the opening `{` brace appears on the **same line** as the selector. Multi-line comma-separated selectors — which are common throughout the codebase — are partially missed, allowing real duplicate class definitions to pass CI undetected.

---

## 📋 Steps to Reproduce

1. Open `scripts/check-duplicates.mjs` and read the class detection pattern:
```js
const classPattern = /^\s*\.([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(?::[a-z-]+)?(?:\s*,\s*\.[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)*\s*\{/;
```
2. This pattern requires `{` to end the line. Open `components/buttons.css` lines 318–320:
```css
.ease-btn,
.ease-btn-hover,
.ease-btn-loading::after {
```
3. Only `.ease-btn-loading` (line 320, which has `{`) is detected. `.ease-btn` and `.ease-btn-hover` on the preceding lines are completely skipped.
4. Add a duplicate `.ease-btn` definition in another component file — `check-duplicates.mjs` reports no duplicate.

---

## ✅ Expected Behavior

All class selectors in a multi-line grouped rule should be individually tracked. A duplicate `.ease-btn` in a second file should be reported as a duplicate regardless of whether `{` is on the same line as `.ease-btn` or two lines below it.

---

## ❌ Actual Behavior

The checker only registers the last selector in a multi-line group (the one on the same line as `{`). All prior selectors in the group are invisible to the duplicate detector, creating false negatives in CI.

---

## 🔗 Reproduction

```bash
# Test the pattern against a multi-line selector group
node -e "
const pattern = /^\s*\.([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(?::[a-z-]+)?(?:\s*,\s*\.[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)*\s*\{/;
console.log(pattern.test('.ease-btn,'));          // false — missed
console.log(pattern.test('.ease-btn-hover,'));    // false — missed
console.log(pattern.test('.ease-btn-loading::after {')); // true — detected
"
```

---

## 🌍 Environment

| Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| EaseMotion CSS version | latest (`main` branch) |
| Affected file | `scripts/check-duplicates.mjs` |

---

## 💡 Suggested Fix

Replace the line-by-line regex approach with a stateful accumulator that collects selector lines until `{` is found, then extracts all class names from the accumulated block:

```js
let pendingSelectors = [];

for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
  const line = lines[i];
  pendingSelectors.push(line.trim());

  if (line.includes('{')) {
    // Extract all class names from accumulated selector block
    const block = pendingSelectors.join(' ');
    const classMatches = block.matchAll(/\.([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)/g);
    for (const match of classMatches) {
      // register match[1] as a found class
    }
    pendingSelectors = [];
  }

  // Also track @keyframes as before
}
```

Alternatively, adopt a CSS AST parser such as `postcss` to walk rules reliably.

