## Feature Name

`lg` breakpoint responsive utilities — `ease-lg-*` classes for screens ≥ 1025px

---

## Description

`core/utilities.css` currently ships two responsive breakpoints:

| Prefix | Breakpoint |
|--------|-----------|
| `ease-sm-*` | `max-width: 768px` (mobile) |
| `ease-md-*` | `769px – 1024px` (tablet) |

There is **no large-screen breakpoint** for desktops (≥ 1025px). This means any layout that needs to behave differently on a wide monitor — a 4-column grid, hiding a sidebar on tablet but showing it on desktop, switching from stacked to side-by-side — requires developers to write their own `@media` query, abandoning the utility-class system entirely.

---

## Why is this useful for EaseMotion CSS?

Most production layouts need at least three breakpoints. Without `lg` utilities, the responsive system is functionally incomplete for real-world use. Adding `ease-lg-*` makes the framework viable for full responsive design without any hand-written media queries.

---

## HTML Snippet

```html
<!-- 1 col on mobile, 2 on tablet, 4 on desktop -->
<div class="ease-grid ease-grid-cols-1 ease-md-grid-cols-2 ease-lg-grid-cols-4 ease-gap-6">
  <div class="ease-card">Item 1</div>
  <div class="ease-card">Item 2</div>
  <div class="ease-card">Item 3</div>
  <div class="ease-card">Item 4</div>
</div>

<!-- Sidebar hidden on mobile/tablet, visible on desktop -->
<aside class="ease-sm-hidden ease-md-hidden ease-lg-block">
  Sidebar content
</aside>

<!-- Stack on mobile, row on desktop -->
<div class="ease-flex ease-flex-col ease-lg-flex-row ease-gap-6">
  <main class="ease-flex-1">Main content</main>
  <aside>Sidebar</aside>
</div>
```

