## Description

The `deleteComment` endpoint only permits the **comment's author** to delete a comment. Post authors have no ability to moderate or remove comments left by other users on their own posts.

This means if someone leaves spam, harassment, or off-topic content on your post, you — as the post owner — are completely unable to remove it. Only the commenter themselves can delete their own comment.

---

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Log in as User A and create a post
2. Log in as User B and leave a comment on User A's post
3. Log back in as User A
4. Attempt to delete User B's comment on your own post
5. The request returns `403 Not Allowed`

---

## Expected Behavior

The post author should be able to delete any comment on their own post, in addition to commenters being able to delete their own comments.

## Actual Behavior

Only the comment's author can delete it. Post authors receive a `403 Not Allowed` response when attempting to remove comments left by others on their own content.

---

## Environment Information

- Component: `server/src/controllers/comment.controller.js`
- Route: `DELETE /api/comments/delete/:commentId`
- Application version: Current main branch

---

## Code Reference

**File:** `server/src/controllers/comment.controller.js` — `deleteComment` (line ~163)

```js
export const deleteComment = asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
    const comment = await Comment.findById(req.params.commentId);
    if (!comment) {
        return res.status(404).json({ message: "Comment not found" });
    }
    // ⚠️ Only the comment's own author is allowed — post author is excluded
    if (comment.author.toString() !== req.user.id) {
        return res.status(403).json({ message: "Not allowed" });
    }
    await comment.deleteOne();
    await Notification.deleteOne({ comment: comment._id, type: "comment" });
    await Post.findByIdAndUpdate(comment.post, { $inc: { commentsCount: -1 } });
    res.json({ success: true });
});
```

---

## Suggested Fix Approach

Fetch the parent post and check if the requesting user is either the comment's author **or** the post's author:

```js
export const deleteComment = asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
    const comment = await Comment.findById(req.params.commentId);
    if (!comment) {
        return res.status(404).json({ message: "Comment not found" });
    }

    const post = await Post.findById(comment.post).select("author");

    const isCommentAuthor = comment.author.toString() === req.user.id;
    const isPostAuthor = post?.author?.toString() === req.user.id;

    if (!isCommentAuthor && !isPostAuthor) {
        return res.status(403).json({ message: "Not allowed" });
    }

    await comment.deleteOne();
    await Notification.deleteOne({ comment: comment._id, type: "comment" });
    await Post.findByIdAndUpdate(comment.post, { $inc: { commentsCount: -1 } });
    res.json({ success: true });
});
```

---

## Additional Context

- This is consistent with how every major social platform (Instagram, Twitter/X, Facebook, LinkedIn) handles comment moderation — post owners always have authority over their own thread.
- The `Post` model already stores `author`, so no schema changes are needed.
- The fix requires one additional DB lookup (`Post.findById`) which is cheap compared to the existing comment lookup already in the same handler.
- The frontend delete button is currently only shown to the comment's own author. Once the backend is updated, the UI should also expose the delete button to the post author.

