## 📌 Bug Description

The export dropdown menu in the dashboard side panel (`role="menu"` with `role="menuitem"` buttons) has correct ARIA markup but lacks the corresponding keyboard interaction pattern required by the WAI-ARIA Menu design pattern.

Currently missing:
1. **No Escape key handling** — pressing Escape while the dropdown is open does nothing
2. **No arrow key navigation** — users cannot move between menu items (MD, TXT, JSON, Copy) using Up/Down arrow keys
3. **No focus management** — when the dropdown opens, focus does not move to the first menu item; when it closes, focus does not return to the trigger button
4. **No close on focus loss** — tabbing out of the dropdown does not close it

The HTML correctly declares `role="menu"` and `role="menuitem"`, which creates an accessibility contract that keyboard behavior will follow the ARIA menu pattern. Failing to implement this is worse than having no roles at all, because assistive technology users expect specific keyboard behavior that is not delivered.

**Affected files:**
- `src/dashboard.ts` — lines 997–1017 (export dropdown toggle logic)
- `src/dashboard.html` — export dropdown section (`id="export-dropdown"`)

```typescript
// dashboard.ts — current implementation (click-only)
exportBtn?.addEventListener("click", () => {
  const isHidden = exportDropdown.hasAttribute("hidden");
  if (isHidden) {
    exportDropdown.removeAttribute("hidden");
    exportBtn.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "true");
  } else {
    exportDropdown.setAttribute("hidden", "");
    exportBtn.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");
  }
});
```

## 🔄 Steps to Reproduce

1. Open the Late-Meet dashboard side panel
2. Tab to the "Export Summary" button
3. Press Enter to open the dropdown
4. Press Escape — nothing happens (dropdown stays open)
5. Press Down Arrow — nothing happens (focus does not move to menu items)
6. Tab — focus moves out of the dropdown without closing it

## ✅ Expected Behavior

Per WAI-ARIA Menu pattern:
- **Enter/Space** on trigger: opens menu, moves focus to first item
- **Escape**: closes menu, returns focus to trigger button
- **Down Arrow**: moves focus to next menu item
- **Up Arrow**: moves focus to previous menu item
- **Tab**: closes menu and moves focus to next focusable element
- **Home/End**: moves focus to first/last menu item

## ❌ Actual Behavior

Only mouse click interaction works. Keyboard users cannot navigate the menu items or dismiss the dropdown. Screen reader users hear the menu role announced but cannot interact with it using expected keyboard patterns.

## 💻 Environment

- Chrome Version: Any
- Operating System: Any
- Extension Version: 1.2.0
- Transcription Provider: N/A (accessibility issue)

## 🛠️ Suggested Fix

Add a `keydown` event listener to the export dropdown wrapper:

```typescript
exportBtn?.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
  if (e.key === "ArrowDown" || e.key === "Enter" || e.key === " ") {
    e.preventDefault();
    openDropdown();
    const firstItem = exportDropdown.querySelector("[role=\"menuitem\"]") as HTMLElement;
    firstItem?.focus();
  }
});

exportDropdown?.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
  const items = Array.from(exportDropdown.querySelectorAll("[role=\"menuitem\"]")) as HTMLElement[];
  const currentIndex = items.indexOf(document.activeElement as HTMLElement);

  switch (e.key) {
    case "Escape":
      e.preventDefault();
      closeDropdown();
      exportBtn?.focus();
      break;
    case "ArrowDown":
      e.preventDefault();
      items[(currentIndex + 1) % items.length]?.focus();
      break;
    case "ArrowUp":
      e.preventDefault();
      items[(currentIndex - 1 + items.length) % items.length]?.focus();
      break;
  }
});
```

Reference: [WAI-ARIA Menu Button Pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/menu-button/)

## 📌 Bug Description

The content script creates a `MutationObserver` that watches `document.body` with `{ childList: true, subtree: true }` to detect when the user navigates from the Google Meet lobby to an actual meeting room. Its sole purpose is to call `injectFloatingButton()` at the right time.

However, this observer **never disconnects** after the button is successfully injected. It continues firing on every single DOM mutation across the entire Google Meet page for the lifetime of the tab.

Google Meet is a complex SPA with constant DOM mutations during active calls (video tile resizing, chat messages, participant indicators, WebRTC status updates, UI animations). This observer fires potentially hundreds of times per second during an active meeting.

**Affected file:** `src/content.ts`

```typescript
const observer = new MutationObserver(() => {
  if (window.location.pathname.length > 5 && !window.location.pathname.includes("/_")) {
    injectFloatingButton();
  }
});

observer.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });
```

The `injectFloatingButton()` function is idempotent (it checks for existing button via `document.getElementById`), but it still queries the DOM on every single mutation callback.

## 🔄 Steps to Reproduce

1. Open a Google Meet tab and join a meeting
2. Open Chrome DevTools Performance tab
3. Record a 10-second trace during an active call
4. Observe frequent MutationObserver callbacks in the flame chart
5. Note that `injectFloatingButton` is called repeatedly even though the button already exists (or was already removed when copilot started)

## ✅ Expected Behavior

The observer should disconnect itself after the floating button is successfully injected. Once the button exists in the DOM, there is no further need to observe body mutations for this purpose.

## ❌ Actual Behavior

The observer runs indefinitely for the entire lifetime of the Google Meet tab, firing on every DOM change. Combined with the `activeSpeakerObserver` (also on `document.body` with `subtree: true`), the content script creates significant overhead on an already CPU-intensive video call page.

## 💻 Environment

- Chrome Version: Any
- Operating System: Any
- Extension Version: 1.2.0
- Transcription Provider: N/A (content script performance issue)

## 🛠️ Suggested Fix

Disconnect the observer after successful button injection:

```typescript
const observer = new MutationObserver(() => {
  if (window.location.pathname.length > 5 && !window.location.pathname.includes("/_")) {
    injectFloatingButton();
    // Button injected — no further need to observe
    const btn = document.getElementById("mc-float-btn");
    if (btn) {
      observer.disconnect();
    }
  }
});
```

Alternative approaches:
- Add a debounce (500ms) to the callback to reduce firing frequency
- Replace with a `setInterval` check every 1–2 seconds (URL changes are infrequent events)
- Use the Navigation API or `popstate` listener instead of a DOM observer for URL-based detection

## �� Bug Description

The waveform visualizer in `offscreen.ts` sends a `chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ type: "WAVEFORM_DATA", buckets })` every **50ms** (`WAVEFORM_INTERVAL_MS = 50`), resulting in **20 IPC messages per second** flowing through the Chrome extension message bus during audio recording.

Each of these messages:
1. Wakes the MV3 service worker if it has been suspended
2. Gets routed through the `background.ts` message handler
3. Falls through to the `default` case and returns `{ success: false, error: "Unknown message type" }`
4. Is separately received by the dashboard via its own `chrome.runtime.onMessage` listener

The background service worker does not use or need waveform data — it is purely a visualization concern for the dashboard side panel. Yet every single message traverses the full service worker message routing pipeline, causing unnecessary wake-ups and wasted processing.

**Affected files:**
- `src/offscreen.ts` — lines 18, 113–131, 404 (sends WAVEFORM_DATA every 50ms)
- `src/background.ts` — message handler default case (rejects WAVEFORM_DATA as unknown)

```typescript
// offscreen.ts
const WAVEFORM_INTERVAL_MS = 50;

function sampleAndSendWaveform() {
  // ...samples audio buffer...
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ type: "WAVEFORM_DATA", buckets }).catch(() => {});
}

waveformTimer = setInterval(sampleAndSendWaveform, WAVEFORM_INTERVAL_MS);
```

## 🔄 Steps to Reproduce

1. Start audio capture on a Google Meet tab
2. Open `chrome://extensions` and inspect the service worker
3. Observe the service worker console logging `Unknown message type` errors 20 times per second
4. Monitor CPU usage — the service worker is constantly being woken

## ✅ Expected Behavior

Waveform data should reach the dashboard without unnecessarily waking or routing through the background service worker. The service worker should remain idle between meaningful events (audio chunks, state updates).

## ❌ Actual Behavior

- 20 messages/second hit the service worker during recording
- Each message is processed, falls through to `default`, and returns an error response
- Service worker cannot suspend during recording due to constant message traffic
- Unnecessary CPU and battery drain

## 💻 Environment

- Chrome Version: Any
- Operating System: Any
- Extension Version: 1.2.0
- Transcription Provider: N/A (IPC architecture issue)

## 🛠️ Suggested Fix

**Option A (minimal):** Add an early-return in `background.ts` message handler for `WAVEFORM_DATA`:
```typescript
case "WAVEFORM_DATA": {
  // Forward to dashboard without processing — waveform is display-only
  sendResponse({ success: true });
  return;
}
```

**Option B (optimal):** Use `BroadcastChannel` API to send waveform data directly from offscreen → dashboard, bypassing the service worker entirely:
```typescript
// offscreen.ts
const waveformChannel = new BroadcastChannel("late-meet-waveform");
waveformChannel.postMessage({ buckets });

// dashboard.ts
const waveformChannel = new BroadcastChannel("late-meet-waveform");
waveformChannel.onmessage = (e) => drawWaveform(e.data.buckets);
```

**Option C (complementary):** Reduce frequency from 50ms to 100ms — 10fps is perceptually smooth for waveform animation and halves the message volume.

**Describe the bug**

The goals sync endpoint (`src/app/api/goals/sync/route.ts`) reads a `repo` value from the database and interpolates it directly into a GitHub Search API query string without any validation or encoding:

```typescript
// Line 83-85: repo comes from DB with no validation
const repo =
  (goal as any).repo ||
  (goal as any).repository ||
  (goal as any).repo_name ||
  null;

// Line 90: directly interpolated into the query
const repoQualifier = repo ? `+repo:${repo}` : "";

// Line 92-93: unsanitized value in URL
const ghRes = await fetch(
  `${GITHUB_API}/search/commits?q=author:${session.githubLogin}${repoQualifier}+author-date:${weekStart}..${weekEnd}&per_page=100&page=${page}`,
  ...
);
```

**Problems:**

1. **Query injection** — If a malicious or malformed `repo` value is stored in the database (e.g., via a compromised admin panel, SQL injection elsewhere, or a migration bug), it can inject arbitrary qualifiers into the GitHub Search API query. For example, a repo value of `owner/repo+author:victim` would alter the search scope.

2. **Type safety bypass** — The code uses `(goal as any)` to access three different possible column names (`repo`, `repository`, `repo_name`), completely bypassing TypeScript's type system. The `.select()` call on line 50 only selects `"id, unit, repo, repository, repo_name"` but there's no guarantee these columns exist or contain valid repository identifiers.

3. **No URL encoding** — Even for legitimate repo names, special characters (spaces, `+`, `#`) would corrupt the query string since the value is not passed through `encodeURIComponent()`.

4. **Inconsistent with contributions route** — The contributions route (`src/app/api/metrics/contributions/route.ts`) uses `URL` and `searchParams.set()` for safe query construction, while this route uses raw string interpolation.

**Expected behavior:**

- The `repo` value should be validated against a pattern like `^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+/[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$` before use
- The query should be constructed using `URL` + `searchParams` (matching the pattern in the contributions route)
- The `(goal as any)` casts should be replaced with a proper typed interface

**Suggested fix:**

```typescript
function isValidRepoQualifier(repo: string): boolean {
  return /^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+\/[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$/.test(repo);
}

// ...
const repoQualifier = repo && isValidRepoQualifier(repo) ? ` repo:${repo}` : "";

const searchUrl = new URL(`${GITHUB_API}/search/commits`);
searchUrl.searchParams.set(
  "q",
  `author:${session.githubLogin}${repoQualifier} author-date:${weekStart}..${weekEnd}`
);
searchUrl.searchParams.set("per_page", "100");
searchUrl.searchParams.set("page", String(page));
```

**File location:** `src/app/api/goals/sync/route.ts` (lines 83–95)

